and the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality, one shows that for any $x\in\Omega$ the evaluation mapping $E_x$ is continuous from $\mathcal H_s(\Omega)$ to $\mathbb R$. Since $\mathcal H_s(\Omega)$ is a Hilbert space, Riesz' theorem gives for each $x\in\Omega$ an element $K_x\in\mathcal H_s(\Omega)$ such that for all $u\in\mathcal H_s(\Omega)$,
\begin{equation} \label{Cani2}\nu(x) = \langle \gamma_D (u) ,\, K_x \rangle_s = \langle M^{\frac{s}{2}} \, \gamma_D (u) ,\,  M^{\frac{s}{2}} \, K_x \rangle_{L^2 (\partial \Omega)}.
\end{equation}
Gathering the Poisson formula above and the preceding identity yields, for any $u\in\mathcal H_s(\Omega)$,
\[
\Big\langle M^{\frac{s}{2}}\gamma_D(u),\, M^{-\frac{s}{2}}\gamma_N\big(G_1(x,\cdot)\big)-M^{\frac{s}{2}}K_x\Big\rangle_{L^2(\partial\Omega)}=0.
