\end{equation}
To prove the main theorem, one then combines the preceding equation with the fact that $\psi$ is a slowly varying function, namely the following lemma.

\begin{lemma}\label{deriv}
Suppose that a function $\psi : \mathbb{N} \to (0, \infty)$ satisfies the following two conditions: 

\begin{itemize}
\item[(I)] there exists $c >0$ such that for all $n \ge 1$ and $k \in [n, 2n]$ 
$$\psi (k) = \psi (n) \big\{ 1 + O \big( (\log n)^{-c} \big) \big\}.$$

\item[(II)] there exists $\epsilon > 0$ such that for all $n \ge 1$ 
$$ \psi (2n) = \psi (n) \Big\{ 1 - \frac{\log 2 }{2 \log n} + O \big( (\log n)^{-1 - \epsilon} \big) \Big\}.$$ 

\end{itemize}
Set $\delta = \min \{ c, \epsilon \}$. Then there exists $a > 0$ such that 
$$\psi (n) = a  (\log n)^{-\frac{1}{2}} \Big\{ 1 + O \Big( \big( \log n \big)^{-\delta} \Big) \Big\},$$

\end{lemma}

To get the preceding equation, let $D_n'$ be the graph distance between $S(n)$ and $S(2n)$ in ${\cal G}_{n,2n}$ and use
\[\psi(n)-\psi(2n)=\frac{\mathbb E(D_n+D_n'-D_{2n})}{2n},\]
so one must analyze $\mathbb E(D_n+D_n'-D_{2n})$. The key geometric input is that if there exist $0\le k\le n\le l\le 2n$ with $S(k)=S(l)$, then as $l-k$ grows, ${\cal G}_{0,2n}$ has a more substantial shortcut, making $D_n+D_n'-D_{2n}$ smaller. Define the maximal intersection length
\[M=\max\{l-k:0\le k\le n\le l\le 2n,\ S(k)=S(l)\},\]
and estimate $\mathbb E(D_n+D_n'-D_{2n})$ by considering cases according to the value of $M$ (actually via a related $L$). Roughly speaking, conditioned on $M=m$, $D_n+D_n'-D_{2n}$ can, with high conditional probability, be approximated by $m\psi(m)$, leading to the approximation
\begin{equation}\label{sumM}
\sum_{m} m \psi (m) P (M=m).
\end{equation}

By giving a sharp estimate of the distribution of $M$, they derive precise upper and lower bounds for this quantity and thus obtain the preceding equation; the needed bounds are provided by the following two propositions.

\begin{proposition}\label{upper}
We have 
$$\psi(n/2) - \psi (n) \le  \frac{\log 2}{2 \log n}  \psi (n/2)  \big\{ 1 + O \big( (\log n)^{-1/100} \big) \big\}.$$
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}\label{lower}
We have 
$$\psi(n/2) - \psi (n) \ge  \frac{\log 2}{2 \log n}  \psi (n/2)  \big\{ 1 + O \big( (\log n)^{-c} \big) \big\}.$$
\end{proposition}

A major difficulty is controlling all error terms so they are no larger than the error in the preceding equation; in particular, estimating the distribution of $M$ with sufficient precision, which is reduced to long-range intersections and handled in the following proposition.

\begin{proposition}\label{fnk}
The following equation holds for all $n, k \ge 2$.
\begin{align*}
f (n;k) = \frac{1}{2 \log n} \, \log \Big[ 1 + \frac{1}{k^{2} +2k } \Big] \,  \bigg\{ 1 + O \bigg( \frac{1}{k(\log n)^{\frac{3}{25}}} \bigg) \bigg\},
\end{align*}
where the constants used in $O$ are universal and do not depend on $n$ and $k$. 
\end{proposition}

