Traditionally, SPDEs are perturbed by a space-time Gaussian white noise in dimension $d=1$, which is a zero-mean Gaussian process $\{B_t(\varphi);t> 0,\varphi \in L^2(\mathbb{R})\}$ with covariance:
\[
\mathbb{E}[B_t(\varphi) B_s(\psi)]=(t \wedge s) \langle \varphi,\psi \rangle_{L^2(\mathbb{R})}.
\]
In higher dimensions, Dalang introduced in \cite{dalang99}, a spatially-homogeneous (or colored) noise, as a zero-mean Gaussian process $\{W_t(\varphi);t\geq 0,\varphi \in \mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R})\}$ with covariance:
\begin{equation}
\label{def-cov-W}
\mathbb{E}[W_t(\varphi) W_s(\psi)]=(t \wedge s) \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} \int_{\mathbb{R}^d}\varphi(x)\psi(y) f(x-y)dxdy,
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ is the set of all rapidly decreasing functions on $\mathbb{R}^d$, and $f:\mathbb{R}^d \to [0,\infty]$ is the Fourier transform of a tempered measure $\mu$. This noise can also be defined as:
\begin{equation}
\label{Gauss-color}
W_t(\varphi)=B_t(\varphi*\kappa) \quad \mbox{for all $t>0$ and $\varphi \in \mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^d)$},
\end{equation}
where the kernel $\kappa$ is chosen such that $k * \widetilde{k}=f$, and $\widetilde{k}(x):=k(-x)$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}^d$.

Let $G$ be the fundamental solution of the wave equation in dimension $d=1$, given by:
\begin{equation}
\label{def-G}
G_t(x)=\frac{1}{2}1_{\{|x|<t\}} \quad \mbox{for all $t>0$ and $x \in \mathbb{R}$.}
\end{equation}

By Lemma 5.5 of \cite{BJ25}, the process $\{u(t,x)\}_{x\in \mathbb{R}}$ is strictly stationary, for any $t>0$.
Moreover, by Theorem 5.6 of \cite{BJ25}, if for some $p\geq 2$, we have
