 \end{equation}is the Gromov-Hausdorff approximation that we want.
Indeed, by Lemma \ref{lem--convexity on y}, and the $C^0_{\loc}$-convergence of $\omega(t)$ to $\omega_{\can}$ on $f^{-1}(Y^{\circ})$ in \eqref{conv}, we know that for any $p,q\in X\setminus \tilde V_{\epsilon}$, we have
 \begin{equation}
 d_t(p,q)\leq d_{\can}(f(p),f(q))+\Psi(t^{-1}|\epsilon).
 \end{equation}
Combining this with \eqref{eq--to prove} then gives
 \begin{equation}
