 Since $L$ is non-split and non-trivial, $S^3\setminus \operatorname{int}(\nu(L))$ is an irreducible, orientable $3$-manifold whose boundary is a union of incompressible tori. By \Cref{laminar}, $B'$ is a laminar branched surface properly embedded in $S^3 \setminus \operatorname{int}(\nu(L))$. By \Cref{bigon}, $\partial \nu(L)\setminus B'$ is a union of bigons. By \Cref{realizable-slopes}, any multislope $(s_1,\ldots,s_n)\in\mathbb{Q}^n$ satisfying $s_i<2g(L_i)-1$ for all $i=1,\ldots,n$ is realized by the boundary train track $B'\cap \partial \nu(L)$.

