is a geometric form.”

Concretely, for a given geometric form $f$, the approach is: (1) “first explicitly construct a degree map $q_{E,E'}$ on $\operatorname{Hom}(E,E')$ such that $f\sim q_{(E\times E', \theta)}$ for some principally polarized abelian surface $(E\times E',\theta)$,” using “the close relationship between the local behavior (assigned characters) of the degree map and the reciprocal form of $f$”; (2) for a given positive definite binary quadratic form $q$, “give a construction of $E$ and $E'$ such that $q\sim q_{E, E'}$”; (3) construct the principal polarization $\theta$, “numerically \dots represented as a $2\times 2$ Hermitian matrix (in terms of the map $\mathbf{D}$ defined in
\begin{equation}
\label{eq: isomorphism_NS(A)}
    \mathbf{D} :\Z \oplus\Z\oplus\Hom(E_1,E_2)\DistTo \NS(A).
\end{equation}
)”; and (4) verify by “computing its refined Humbert invariant.” A key algorithmic ingredient is “finding a prime number represented by a given positive definite primitive ternary quadratic form”; GRH is assumed only “to utilize a sharper bound,” while “the correctness of the algorithm's output does not depend on the GRH.”

