The purpose of this paper is to prove this elegant geometric inequality \eqref{MT} by relating it to the positivity of the hyperbolic Hamiltonian mass \cite{CCQW25} in the $(2+1)$-dimensional gravity theory \cite{St63}. The main result in this work might be viewed as an analogue of the positivity of the Brown-York mass \cite{BY93} in general relativity firstly proved by Shi and Tam in their seminal work \cite{ST-JDG02}.

Motivated by the hyperbolic version of the mass expression in \cite[Line 8 in front of Section 2, Page 2358]{ST-CQG07}, we define the Brown-York-Shi-Tam (BYST) quasi-local mass for the boundary curve $\Sigma$ as
\be\begin{split}
\m_{BYST}(\Sigma):& =\frac{1}{\pi}\int_\Sigma \big(\hat{k}-k\big)\sqrt{1+r_0^2}\d s\\
&=\frac{1}{\pi}\int_{0}^{2\pi}\big(\hat{k}-k\big)r_0\sqrt{1+r_0^2}\d \varphi.
\end{split}
\ee
Here $k$ and $\hat k$ are the geodesic curvatures with respect to the physical metric $g$ and the reference metric $g_{-1}$ respectively. The inequality \eqref{MT} is equivalent to the positivity of $\m_{BYST}$, i.e., $\m_{BYST}(\Sigma)\geq 0$.

Let $\tilde{M}_r \subset \tilde{M}$ be the region with $\partial \tilde{M}_r=\Sigma_r$. On $\tilde{M}_r\setminus \Omega$, where the scalar curvature $R(g^u)=-2$, one has
