full_context:
The $L^2$-theory for the $\overline{\partial}$-operator has become an important and indispensable area in complex analysis through H\"{o}rmander's foundational work on $L^2$-estimates and existence theorems (see \cite{Hor65,Hor90}), as well as the related studies of Andreotti-Vesentini (see \cite{AV65}).
Subsequently, $L^2$-extension theorems (see \cite{OT87}) were introduced by Ohsawa-Takegoshi, and in recent years, complex geometry has further developed through applications of such theorems together with singular Hermitian metrics on vector bundles and its positivity (see \cite{Dem90,AS95,Tak98,BP08,Ber09,PT18,DNWZ23,Li24,IMW26}), 
and singular Hermitian metrics and multiplier ideal sheaves have become increasingly important.
While this theory is very well developed on complex manifolds, constructing an appropriate $L^2$-theory on complex spaces still presents various difficulties, 
although it has been partially achieved in the case of smooth Hermitian metrics (see \cite{Rup14}). 
Central to these issues are the $L^2$-Dolbeault fine resolution and isomorphism, and $L^2$-estimates. 
In this paper, we study these fundamental problems for holomorphic line bundles equipped with singular Hermitian metrics on complex spaces, 
using the strong openness property, Demailly's approximation, embedding theorems, and related techniques. 
As an application, we also establish corresponding vanishing theorems.

Throughout this paper, all complex spaces are assumed to be reduced without further notice.
Let $X$ be a complex space of pure dimension $n$. 
The \textit{Grauert}-\textit{Riemenschneider canonical sheaf} $\omega_X^{GR}$ on $X$ is defined by $\Gamma(U,\omega_X^{GR})=\{s\in\Gamma(U\capX_{reg},\omega_{X_{reg}})\mid i^{n^2}s\wedge\overline{s}\in L^1_{loc}(U)\}$
for any open subset $U\subset X$. 
For each locally integrable function $\varphi\in L^1_{loc}(X)$, we denote by $\omega_X^{GR}(\varphi)$ the $\mathcal{O}_X$-sheaf on $X$ defined by 
\begin{align*}
 \Gamma(U,\omega_X^{GR}(\varphi))=\{s\in\Gamma(U\capX_{reg},\omega_{X_{reg}})\mid i^{n^2}s\wedge\overline{s}\cdot e^{-2\varphi}\in L^1_{loc}(U)\}
\end{align*}
for any open subset $U\subset X$ (see \cite[Definition 2.3]{Li24}). In particular, for a singular Hermitian metric $h$ on a holomorphic line bundle, we defines $\omega_X^{GR}(h):=\omega_X^{GR}(\varphi)$ if $h$ can be written locally as $h=e^{-2\varphi}$.
Furthermore, for any quasi-plurisubharmonic function $\varphi:X\longrightarrow [-\infty,+\infty)$, the $\mathcal{O}_X$-sheaf $\omega_X^{GR}(\varphi)$ satisfies the functoriality property $\omega_X^{GR}(\varphi)=\pi_*(K_{\widetilde{X}}\otimes\mathscr{I}(\pi^*\varphi))$ (see \cite[Proposition 5.8]{Dem12}), 
where $\pi:\widetilde{X}\longrightarrow X$ is any resolusion of singularities. 
In particular, if $X$ is smooth, then $\omega_X^{GR}(\varphi)=K_X\otimes\mathscr{I}(\varphi)$ by definition and $\pi:\widetilde{X}\longrightarrow X$ can be taken to be any proper modification.

We first present the following $L^2$-Dolbeault fine resolution and isomorphism.
Let $L\longrightarrow X$ be a holomorphic line bundle on a complex space.
For singular Hermitian metrics $h$ on $L$ (see Definition \ref{Definition: singular Hermitian metrics on cpx sp}), we define the subsheaf $\mathscr{L}^{p,q}_{L,h}$ on $X$ to be the sheaf of germs of $(p,q)$-forms $u$ with values in $L$ and with measurable coefficients such that both $|u|^2_h$ and $|\overline{\partial} u|^2_h$ are locally integrable on the regular locus (see Section \ref{Section: dbar-operator and L2-Dolbeault complexes}). 
Here $\overline{\partial}$-operator is considered in the sense of distribution on $X_{reg}$.

\begin{definition}\label{Definition: singular Hermitian metrics on cpx sp}
    Let $X$ be a complex space and $L\longrightarrow X$ be a holomorphic line bundle. 
    We say that $h$ is a singular Hermitian metric on $L$ if for any smooth Hermitian metric $h_0$ on $L$, there exists a locally integrable function $\varphi$ on $X$, i.e., $\varphi\in L^1_{loc}(X)$, such that $h=h_0e^{-2\varphi}$ on $X$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}\label{Theorem: L2-Dolbeault isomorphism}
 Let $X$ be a complex space of pure dimension $n$, $\pi:\tx\longrightarrow X$ be a canonical desingularization and $L\longrightarrow X$ be a holomorphic line bundle with a singular Hermitian metric $h$.
 If any local weight function of $h$ on $X$ is quasi-plurisubharmonic, then the $L^2$-Dolbeault complex $(\pi_*\scr{L}^{n,*}_{\pi^*L,\pi^*h},\pi_*\dbar)$ 
 is a fine resolution of \begin{align*}
 \ogr(h)\otimes\cal{O}_X(L)\cong\pi_*(\omega_{\tx}\otimes\cal{O}_{\tx}(\pi^*L)\otimes\scr{I}(\pi^*h)).
 \end{align*}
 Thus, we have the following $L^2$-Dolbeault isomorphism 
 \begin{align*}
 H^q(X,\ogr(h)\otimes\cal{O}_X(L))\cong H^q(\tx,K_{\tx}\otimes\pi^*L\otimes\scr{I}(\pi^*h)) 
 \end{align*}
 for any $q\geq0$. In particular, the isomorphism 
 \begin{align*}
 H^q(U,\ogr(h)\otimes\cal{O}_X(L))\cong H^q(\pi^{-1}(U),K_{\tx}\otimes\pi^*L\otimes\scr{I}(\pi^*h))
 \end{align*}
 holds for any open subset $U\subset X$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}\label{Theorem: higher direct image vanishing}
 Let $X$ be a complex space, $\pi:\tx\longrightarrow X$ be a canonical desingularization and $L\longrightarrow X$ be a holomorphic line bundle with a singular Hermitian metric $h$. 
 If any local weight function of $h$ on $X$ is quasi-plurisubharmonic, then we have the higher direct image vanishing 
 \begin{align*}
 R^q\pi_*(K_{\tx}\otimes\pi^*L\otimes\scr{I}(\pi^*h))=0 
 \end{align*}
 for any $q>0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}\label{Theorem: Nadel vanishing on Moishezon}
 Let $X$ be a compact complex space of pure dimension $n$ and $L\longrightarrow X$ be a holomorphic line bundle. 
 If $L$ is big, then $X$ is Moishezon and there exists a singular Hermitian metric $h$ on $L$ such that $\iO{L,h}\geq\gamma$ on $\reg$ in the sense of currents for some Hermitian metric $\gamma$ on $X$, 
 and we have the following cohomology vanishing 
 \begin{align*}
 H^q(X,\ogr(h)\otimes\cal{O}_X(L))=0 
 \end{align*} 
 for any $q>0$, without assuming the existence of a \kah metric. 
 Here, if $X$ is normal, then we can take the singular Hermitian metric $h$ with $\iO{L,h}\geq\gamma$ on $X$ in the sense of currents, i.e., $h$ is singular positive. 
\end{theorem}

Let $X$ be a complex space of pure dimension $n$. 
As in the smooth case, the sheaf $\scr{D}'^{\,p,q}_X$ of currents of bidegree $(p,q)$ on $X$ is by definition the dual of $\scr{D}^{\,p,q}_X$ which is the space of smooth differential forms of bidegree $(p,q)$ with compact support. 
Given a local embedding $\iota:U\hookrightarrow \bb{C}^N$, thus, the currents $T\in\scr{D}'^{\,p,q}_X$ precisely correspond, via $T\longmapsto\iota_*T$, to the currents of bidegree $(N-n+p,N-n+q)$ in the ambient space that vanish on all test forms $\phi$ such that $\iota^*\phi=0$ on $\reg$ (see \cite{Dem85,AS12}).
Here, the current $\iota_*T$ is defined by \begin{align*}
 \lara{\iota_*T}{\tl{\phi}}:=\lara{T}{\iota^*\tl{\phi}}=\int_{U_{reg}} T\wedge\iota^*\tl{\phi},
\end{align*}
for any test forms $\tl{\phi}$ on $\bb{C}^N$. 
Furthermore, the positivity of the current $T\in\scr{D}'^{\,p,p}_X$ is also defined as in the smooth case, and this is equivalent to the positivity of $\iota_*T$. In other words, a current $T\in\scr{D}'^{\,p,p}_X$ is said to be \textit{positive}, i.e., $T\geq0$ \textit{on} $X$ \textit{in the sense of currents}, if $\lara{T}{\phi}\geq0$ for any strongly positive test form $\phi\in\scr{D}^{n-p,n-p}_X(X)$ on $X$, which can be written as $\phi=\rho\cdot i^{(n-p)^2}\eta\wedge\overline{\eta}$ suitable forms $\rho\in\scr{D}(X,\bb{R}_{\geq0})$ and $\eta\in\cal{E}_X^{n-p,0}(X)$.
Here, $\cal{E}_X^{p,q}$ denotes the sheaf of smooth differential forms. 
Note that, if $\varphi\in L^1_{loc}(U)$ then $[\varphi]\in\scr{D}_X'(U)$ and $\iota_*[\varphi]\in\scr{D}'(\bb{C}^N)$; however, in general $\iota_*\varphi$ is not locally integrable. 
Moreover, the form $\idd\varphi$ is defined on $U$ as a current by \begin{align*}
 \lara{\idd\varphi}{\phi}:=\int_{U_{reg}}\varphi\,\idd\phi,
\end{align*}
for any test form $\phi\in\scr{D}^{n-1,n-1}(U)$, i.e., $\idd\varphi\in\scr{D}_X'^{\,1,1}(U)$. 
In particular, it follows that the curvature $\iO{L,h}$ associated with a singular Hermitian metric $h$ on $L$ is always well-defined as a current, 
and the current $\iO{L,h}$ always has locally finite mass, i.e., \begin{align*}
 ||\iO{L,h}||_{\omega}(K):=\int_K\iO{L,h}\wedge\omega^{n-1}=\sum_{\alpha\in\Lambda}\int_{U_\alpha\cap K}\varphi_\alpha\,\idd\omega^{n-1}<+\infty,
\end{align*}
for any Hermitian metric $\omega$ and any compact subset $K\Subset X$, here $\varphi_\alpha\in L^1_{loc}(U_\alpha)$ is local weight function of $h$.
Therefore, the following holds by the Skoda-El Mir extension theorem (see \cite[Chapter III, Theorem 2.3]{Dem-book}, \cite[Theorem 1.18]{Dem12}).

In this section, we provide proofs of Theorems \ref{Theorem: fine Dolbeault resolution} and \ref{Theorem: L2-Dolbeault isomorphism}.
Let $X$ be a complex space of pure dimension $n$ with a Hermitian metric $\omega$, $L\longrightarrow X$ be a holomorphic line bundle with a singular Hermitian metric $h$ and $U\subset X$ be an open subset. 
Recall that 
\begin{align*}
 L^{2,loc}_{p,q}(U,L,h):=\Bigl\{u\in L^{2,loc}_{p,q}(\ureg,L,h)\,\Big|\, u|_{K_{reg}}\in L^2_{p,q}(K_{reg},L,h),\forall\,K\Subset U\Bigr\}.
\end{align*} 
It is easy to check that the presheaves given as $\scr{L}^{2,loc}_{p,q}(L,h)(U):=L^{2,loc}_{p,q}(U,L,h)$ are already sheaves $\scr{L}^{2,loc}_{p,q}(L,h)\longrightarrow X$. 
On $L^{2,loc}_{p,q}(U,L,h)$, we denote by 
\begin{align*}
 \dbar(U):L^{2,loc}_{p,q}(U,L,h)\longrightarrow L^{2,loc}_{p,q+1}(U,L,h)
\end{align*}
the $\dbar$-operator in the sense of distributions on $\ureg=U\setminus\xs$ which is closed and densely defined. 
This $\dbar$-operator is often denoted by $\dbar_w$ or $\dbar_{max}$.
Since $\dbar$ is a local operator, we can define the presheaves of germs of forms in the domain of $\dbar$, 
\begin{align*}
 \scr{L}^{p,q}_{L,h}:=\scr{L}^{2,loc}_{p,q}(L,h)\cap \dbar^{-1}\scr{L}^{2,loc}_{p,q+1}(L,h),
\end{align*}
given by $\scr{L}^{p,q}_{L,h}(U)=\scr{L}^{2,loc}_{p,q}(L,h)(U)\cap\rom{Dom}\,\dbar(U)$. 
This sheaf $\scr{L}^{p,q}_{L,h}$ is well known when $X$ is smooth (see \cite{Dem12,Wat25a}), and admits various types of $L^2$-Dolbeault resolutions (see \cite[Theorem 5.3]{Wat25a}, \cite[Theorem 3.7]{Wat26}).
Furthermore, it is easy to see that the sheaves $\scr{L}^{p,q}_{L,h}$ admit partitions of unity, then we obtain sequences of fine sheaves 
\begin{align*}
 \scr{L}^{p,0}_{L,h}\overset{\dbar}{\longrightarrow}\scr{L}^{p,1}_{L,h}\overset{\dbar}{\longrightarrow}\scr{L}^{p,2}_{L,h}\overset{\dbar}{\longrightarrow}\cdots.
\end{align*}

\begin{theorem}\label{Theorem: isom of cohomology in compact spaces}
 Let $X$ be a compact complex space of pure dimension $n$, $\pi:\tx\longrightarrow X$ be any resolusion of singularities and $L\longrightarrow X$ be a holomorphe line bundle with a singular Hermitian metric $h$. 
 If any local weight function of $h$ on $X$ is quasi-plurisubharmonic, then the pullback of forms under $\pi$ induces a natural isomorphism 
 \begin{align*}
 \pi^*:H^{n,q}_{L^2}(\reg,L;\omega,h)_{max}\overset{\cong}{\longrightarrow} H^q(\tx,K_{\tx}\otimes\pi^*L\otimes\scr{I}(\pi^*h)).
 \end{align*}
 for any Hermitian metric $\omega$ on $X$ and any $q\geq0$, where we have \begin{align*}
 H^{n,q}_{L^2}(\reg,L;\omega,h)_{max}=H^q(X,\ogr(h)\otimes\cal{O}_X(L)). 
 \end{align*}
\end{theorem}

post_theorem_intro_text:
\begin{theorem}\label{Theorem: L2-Dolbeault isomorphism}
    Let $X$ be a complex space of pure dimension $n$, $\pi:\widetilde{X}\longrightarrow X$ be a canonical desingularization and $L\longrightarrow X$ be a holomorphic line bundle with a singular Hermitian metric $h$.
    If any local weight function of $h$ on $X$ is quasi-plurisubharmonic, then the $L^2$-Dolbeault complex $(\pi_*\mathscr{L}^{n,*}_{\pi^*L,\pi^*h},\pi_*\overline{\partial})$ 
    is a fine resolution of     \begin{align*}
        \omega_X^{GR}(h)\otimes\mathcal{O}_X(L)\cong\pi_*(\omega_{\widetilde{X}}\otimes\mathcal{O}_{\widetilde{X}}(\pi^*L)\otimes\mathscr{I}(\pi^*h)).
    \end{align*}
    Thus, we have the following $L^2$-Dolbeault isomorphism 
    \begin{align*}
        H^q(X,\omega_X^{GR}(h)\otimes\mathcal{O}_X(L))\cong H^q(\widetilde{X},K_{\widetilde{X}}\otimes\pi^*L\otimes\mathscr{I}(\pi^*h)) 
    \end{align*}
    for any $q\geq0$. In particular, the isomorphism 
    \begin{align*}
        H^q(U,\omega_X^{GR}(h)\otimes\mathcal{O}_X(L))\cong H^q(\pi^{-1}(U),K_{\widetilde{X}}\otimes\pi^*L\otimes\mathscr{I}(\pi^*h))
    \end{align*}
    holds for any open subset $U\subset X$.
\end{theorem}

Various versions of Theorem \ref{Theorem: fine Dolbeault resolution} are known when $X$ is smooth (see \cite{Dem12,Wat25a,Wat26}). 
For spaces with singularities, results corresponding to smooth Hermitian metrics for the line bundles in Theorems \ref{Theorem: fine Dolbeault resolution} and \ref{Theorem: L2-Dolbeault isomorphism} are known (see \cite[Theorem 2.1 and 3.1]{Rup14}).
Related results include the Dolbeault fine resolution for the sheaf of $(0,q)$-currents on complex spaces (see \cite{AS12}).
These theorems are obtained by using the Takegoshi-type higher direct image vanishing below, together with the local $L^2$-$\overline{\partial}$ cohomology vanishing on the regular locus derived from $L^2$-estimates.

\begin{theorem}\label{Theorem: higher direct image vanishing}
    Let $X$ be a complex space, $\pi:\widetilde{X}\longrightarrow X$ be a canonical desingularization and $L\longrightarrow X$ be a holomorphic line bundle with a singular Hermitian metric $h$. 
    If any local weight function of $h$ on $X$ is quasi-plurisubharmonic, then we have the higher direct image vanishing 
    \begin{align*}
        R^q\pi_*(K_{\widetilde{X}}\otimes\pi^*L\otimes\mathscr{I}(\pi^*h))=0 
    \end{align*}
    for any $q>0$.
\end{theorem}

The proof requires the Negativity lemma and the strong openness property to compensate for the degenerate positivity and to preserve the ideal sheaf.
Subsequently, to establish the vanishing theorem, we present the following global $L^2$-estimates on the regular locus of a weakly pseudoconvex complex space.
Here, weakly pseudoconvexity includes both compact and Stein cases and forms the broadest and highly significant class among the complex-geometric objects that can be treated.
In fact, every complex Lie group is always weakly pseudoconvex (see \cite{Kaz73}).

\begin{theorem}\label{Theorem: global L2-estimates}
    Let $X$ be a weakly pseudoconvex K\"{a}hler  complex space of pure dimension $n$ and $L\longrightarrow X$ be a holomorphic line bundle with a singular Hermitian metric $h$. 
    We assume that $h$ is singular positive, then $i\Theta_{L,h}\geq2\varepsilon\omega$ on $X$ in the sense of currents for some positive smooth function $\varepsilon:X\longrightarrow\mathbb{R}_{>0}$ and a K\"{a}hler  metric $\omega$.     For any $q>0$ and for any $f\in L^{2,loc}_{n,q}(X,L,h)$ satisfying $\overline{\partial} f=0$ on $X_{reg}$ and $\int_X\frac{1}{\varepsilon}|f|^2_{h,\omega}\,dV_\omega<+\infty$, 
    there exists $u\in L^2_{n,q-1}(X,L;\omega,h)$ satisfying $\overline{\partial} u=f$ on $X_{reg}$ and the global $L^2$-estimate
    \begin{align*}
        \int_X|u|^2_{h,\omega}\,dV_\omega\leq\frac{1}{q}\int_X\frac{1}{\varepsilon}|f|^2_{h,\omega}\,dV_\omega.
    \end{align*}

    Furthermore, if $X$ is not necessarily K\"{a}hler  and $\omega$ is taken simply as a Hermitian metric, then the same global $L^2$-estimate as above holds only in the case $q=1$.
\end{theorem}

Together with Theorem \ref{Theorem: fine Dolbeault resolution}, we obtain the following.

\begin{theorem}\label{Theorem: Nadel vanishing on w.p.c. cpx sp}
    Let $X$ be a weakly pseudoconvex K\"{a}hler  complex space of pure dimension $n$ and $L\longrightarrow X$ be a holomorphic line bundle with a singular Hermitian metric $h$. 
    If $h$ is singular positive, then we have the following vanishing 
    \begin{align*}
        H^q(X,\omega_X^{GR}(h)\otimes\mathcal{O}_X(L))=0 
    \end{align*}
    for any $q>0$. 
    Furthermore, if $X$ is not necessarily K\"{a}hler, then we have only the $1$-st cohomology vanishing $H^1(X,\omega_X^{GR}(h)\otimes\mathcal{O}_X(L))=0$.
\end{theorem}

The Nadel vanishing theorem \ref{Theorem: Nadel vanishing on w.p.c. cpx sp} is already mentioned in \cite[Remark 5.17]{Dem12}, but there appears to be a nontrivial technical gap in the argument (see Remark \ref{Remark: about Remark 5.17 on Dem12}), and here we provide a complete proof.
When the complex space is compact, by applying Theorem \ref{Theorem: L2-Dolbeault isomorphism}, we can obtain the higher cohomology vanishing without assuming the existence of a K\"{a}hler  metric, which is a generalization of Nadel vanishing. 

\begin{theorem}\label{Theorem: Nadel vanishing on Moishezon}
    Let $X$ be a compact complex space of pure dimension $n$ and $L\longrightarrow X$ be a holomorphic line bundle. 
    If $L$ is big, then $X$ is Moishezon and there exists a singular Hermitian metric $h$ on $L$ such that $i\Theta_{L,h}\geq\gamma$ on $X_{reg}$ in the sense of currents for some Hermitian metric $\gamma$ on $X$, 
    and we have the following cohomology vanishing 
    \begin{align*}
        H^q(X,\omega_X^{GR}(h)\otimes\mathcal{O}_X(L))=0 
    \end{align*} 
    for any $q>0$, without assuming the existence of a K\"{a}hler  metric. 
    Here, if $X$ is normal, then we can take the singular Hermitian metric $h$ with $i\Theta_{L,h}\geq\gamma$ on $X$ in the sense of currents, i.e., $h$ is singular positive. 
\end{theorem}

Recently, even on complex spaces, the \textit{Nadel}-\textit{type multiplier ideal sheaf} $\widehat{\mathscr{I}}(\varphi)$ (see \cite[Definition 3.2]{Li24}) has been introduced as an appropriate multiplier ideal sheaf by taking singularities into account via Ohsawa's extension measure, 
and it is known that $\omega_X^{GR}(\varphi)=\omega_X\otimes\widehat{\mathscr{I}}(\varphi)$ holds (see \cite[Remark 3.3]{Li24}) if the complex space is locally a complete intersection (not necessarily normal).
Here, $\omega_X$ denotes the \textit{dualizing sheaf}, which is defined by $\Gamma(U,\omega_X)=\mathrm{Ext}^{N-n}_{\mathcal{O}_D}(\mathcal{O}_U,\Omega^N_D)|_U$ for any open set $U$ admitting a holomorphic closed embedding $\iota_U:U\hookrightarrow\mathbb{C}^N$, and satisfies $\omega_X^{GR}\subset\omega_X$.

As an application, we obtain the following Kawamata-Viehweg type vanishing theorem without assuming conditions on the singularities such as klt, 
or projectivity, in particular the existence of a K\"{a}hler  metric.

\begin{corollary}\label{Corollary: Kawamata-Viehweg type vanishing}
    Let $X$ be a compact Moishezon space of pure dimension $n$ and $L\longrightarrow X$ be a holomorphic line bundle. 
    If $L$ is nef and big, then we have the following vanishing 
    \begin{align*}
        H^q(X,\omega_X^{GR}\otimes\mathcal{O}_X(L))=0 
    \end{align*}
    for any $q>0$.
\end{corollary}

For a normal complex space $X$, if $X$ or the pair $(X,\Delta)$ is klt, then $X$ has rational singularities and hence $\omega_X=\omega_X^{GR}$; 
therefore, Corollary \ref{Corollary: Kawamata-Viehweg type vanishing} is a generalization of the Kawamata-Viehweg vanishing theorem.

