expanded_sketch:
Let $G$ be a $(P_7,C_4,C_6)$-free (equivalently, $(P_7$, even-hole)-free) graph. The strategy is to split into two cases depending on whether $G$ contains an induced $C_7$.

1) If $G$ contains an induced $C_7$, then “based on a structural result of Penev \cite{Penev25},” one establishes the main theorem in this case (this case is described as “easy”).

2) Otherwise assume $G$ is $C_7$-free. Using “a classical result on chordal graphs by Dirac,” namely the following lemma:
\begin{lemma}[\cite{Dirac61}]\label{lem:chordal has simplicial vertices}
    For every chordal graph $G$ that is not a complete graph, there are two non-adjacent simplicial vertices of $G$.
\end{lemma}
one may assume $G$ contains an induced $C_5$ (this is the “difficult” case). Define and analyze a “nice blowup” $H$ of an induced $C_5$ “that mimics the structure of an induced $C_5$,” and partition $V(G)\setminus V(H)$ into subsets $A_0,A_1,A_2,A_3,A_5$ according to their neighborhoods in $H$. After establishing structural properties of these subsets, prove the main theorem by handling the remaining configurations via three subcases:

- $A_1\neq\emptyset$ (next, the first subcase),
- $A_1=\emptyset$ and $A_2\neq\emptyset$ (then, the second subcase),
- $A_1=A_2=\emptyset$ but $A_3\neq\emptyset$ (finally, the third subcase).

These three subcases together complete the proof of the main theorem.

Technical tools highlighted for executing this plan include “reducible structures” (clique cutsets, small vertices, and $(p,q)$-good subgraphs), and it suffices to prove the main theorem for graphs $G$ without those structures, by the following lemma:
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:basic graphs}
    Let $\mathcal{G}$ be a graph class. If every basic graph $G'$ in $\mathcal{G}$ has $\chi(G')\le \ceil{\frac{5\omega(G')}{4}}$, then every graph $G$ in $\mathcal{G}$ has $\chi(G)\le \ceil{\frac{5\omega(G)}{4}}$.
\end{lemma}
The proof then uses a “clique cutset argument” (with “nice partitions” and an “Infinite Descent”-type minimality contradiction), a “small vertex argument” (showing certain neighborhoods are cliques to force multiple disjoint ‘large’ cliques and hence a contradiction to $\omega(G)$), a “good subgraph argument” (using $(4,5)$-good subgraphs, notably later in the $C_7$ case and in the third subcase above), and finally a “pre-coloring argument” where one “give[s] an explicit proper coloring using $\ceil{\frac{5}{4}\omega(G)}$ colors” by first coloring a subgraph and then reducing the remainder to coloring hyperholes, using the following lemma:
\begin{lemma}[\cite{NS01}]\label{lem:hyperhole}
    For any $k$-hyperhole $H$, $\chi (H)=\max \left \{\omega(H), \ceil{\frac{|V(H)|}{\alpha(H)}} \right \}$.
\end{lemma}

