\end{proof}

Since column $j_1$ is not packed, there must be some $i'$ such that $(i',j_1) \notin D(w)$ but $(i'+1,j_1) \in D(w)$. Note that this implies $w(i')<j_1$ and $i'+1<w^{-1}(j_1)$. If $i'<i_1$, then $w(i'),w(i_1),j_1,w(i_2),j_2$ is a $13254$ pattern in the one-line notation of $w$. Otherwise, if $i_1<i'$, then because $(i'+1,j_1)\in D(w)$, we must have $j_1<w(i'+1)$. In the latter case, we have $i_1<i'<i'+1<w^{-1}(j_1)<i_2<w^{-1}(j_2)$ and either $w(i')<j_1<w(i'+1)<w(i_1)<j_2<w(i_2)$, $w(i')<j_1<w(i_1)<w(i'+1)<j_2<w(i_2)$, $w(i')<j_1<w(i_1)<j_2<w(i'+1)<w(i_2)$, or $w(i')<j_1<w(i_1)<j_2<w(i_2)<w(i'+1)$. In other words, $w$ contains at least one of the patterns $413265$, $314265$, $315264$, or $316254$. Since, $413265$ and $314265$ both contain $13254$ patterns, we conclude that $w$ contains either a $13254$, $315264$, or $316254$ pattern.
\end{proof}
